{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"01","section":"06","chapter_title":"Digital Citizenship and AI Readiness","title":"Accessibility Basics","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting accessibility basics to privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness","accessibility","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":6,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/01/05"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_calibration"]},"content":"# Accessibility Basics\n\n![Accessibility Basics textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/01-06-accessibility-basics.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"PHOJwnSV6t4\" title=\"Universal Design for Learning and CAST\" caption=\"Watch before the accessibility revision. Use the UDL frame to check whether access is built into the design rather than added as a final polish step.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen you make a slide, a document, or a web page, some of your audience will use a screen reader, some cannot distinguish red from green, and some are reading on a dim phone in bright sun. Accessibility is the practice of making sure your content works for all of them, not just for a sighted reader on a good monitor. It is not charity or an afterthought; it is part of doing the job correctly, and in many settings it is a legal requirement.\n\nThe work product is a before-after accessibility pass on a real artifact, where each fix maps to a specific, checkable standard. The WCAG guidelines turn \"make it accessible\" into concrete, testable items: does the image have alt text, does the page use real headings, does meaning survive without color, does the text have enough contrast.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write alt text that conveys an image's meaning, not just its file name.\n- Use real heading styles so assistive technology can navigate the structure.\n- Ensure no information is carried by color alone.\n- Check text contrast against the WCAG threshold and fix failures.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nJordan finishes a slide titled \"Club Growth\" with a bar chart and a list of steps. It looks clean. But the chart's alt text is `Chart1.png`, the title is just large bold text rather than a heading, the required steps are marked only in red, and the body text is light gray on white. To a sighted reader on a laptop, none of this is visible. To a student using a screen reader, the chart is meaningless and the title is not announced; to a colorblind classmate, \"required\" is invisible; to anyone in bright light, the gray text disappears.\n\nThe slide is not broken for Jordan, which is exactly why the problem is easy to miss. Accessibility errors are usually invisible to the person who made them.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAccessibility is the principle that content should not depend on a single way of perceiving it. A screen reader needs text where you put an image; a colorblind reader needs a second cue besides color; a low-vision reader needs contrast. Each need maps to a concrete WCAG check.\n\n| Check | What it fixes | How to verify it |\n|-------|---------------|------------------|\n| Alt text | Images for screen-reader users | Read the alt aloud: does it convey the meaning? |\n| Heading structure | Navigation for assistive tech | Use real heading styles, not big bold text |\n| Color independence | Meaning for colorblind users | Remove color: is the info still there? |\n| Contrast ratio | Readability for low vision | Check against the WCAG 4.5:1 ratio for body text |\n\nTwo ideas make these checks make sense. First, alt text should describe the image's function in context, not its appearance: a chart's alt text is the conclusion the chart shows (\"membership rose from 20 to 55\"), not \"a bar chart.\" Second, a real heading is structural, not visual: applying the `Heading 1` style lets a screen reader announce and jump to it, while text that is merely large and bold looks like a heading but is invisible to navigation. Building access in from the start (the Universal Design for Learning idea) is far easier than retrofitting it at the end.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A slide marks every required reading in red and adds no other label. A classmate who cannot tell red from green opens it. What does the color-independence check say?\" explanation=\"WCAG color-independence means meaning must survive when color is removed. A second cue, such as the word Required next to each item, carries the meaning for a colorblind reader; brightening or changing the hue does not, because the information is still encoded only in color.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It passes: red is a strong, attention-grabbing color\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It fails: add a non-color cue such as the word Required so meaning does not depend on color\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It passes once the red is made brighter\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Swap red for green and it is fixed\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nA first draft of an accessibility claim often hides the real work:\n\n> I made the slide more accessible.\n\nThat sentence is too thin because it names no check and no change. A reviewer cannot confirm it. A strong record shows each WCAG check, the before state, and the specific fix, so accessibility becomes a list of confirmable items rather than a claim.\n\n### Worked Example: fixing one slide for accessibility, step by step\n\nTake a single slide titled \"Club Growth\" and run an accessibility pass.\n\n1. **Alt text.** Before: the chart's alt text is `Chart1.png`. After: `Bar chart showing club membership rising from 20 members in fall to 55 in spring.`\n2. **Heading structure.** Before: the title is just 28pt bold text. After: apply the real `Heading 1` style so screen readers announce it as a heading.\n3. **Color is not the only cue.** Before: required steps are shown only in red. After: keep the red but add the word `Required` next to each, so meaning does not depend on color.\n4. **Contrast check.** Before: light gray text on white. After: switch to dark gray on white to clear the contrast threshold.\n\nResult: the slide looks similar but now passes four concrete checks, each one a change a reviewer can confirm rather than a claim that it is \"more accessible.\"\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run four WCAG checks on the Club Growth slide\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The chart alt text is currently \\\"Chart1.png.\\\" What does functional alt text say instead?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A bar chart with blue bars\",\"Chart1.png is fine for screen readers\",\"Bar chart showing club membership rising from 20 in fall to 55 in spring\",\"An image of a graph\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Functional alt text states the image purpose, the conclusion it shows, not its appearance. A screen-reader user needs the trend, not the file name.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The title is 28pt bold text. Why is applying the real Heading 1 style different from just making text big and bold?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is not different; both look like headings\",\"Big bold text is invisible to screen-reader navigation, while a real heading is announced and jumpable\",\"Heading 1 only changes the color\",\"Bold text is more accessible than headings\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A real heading is structural: assistive tech can announce and jump to it. Text that is merely large looks like a heading but cannot be navigated.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Required steps are shown only in red. What fix keeps the meaning for a colorblind reader?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Make the red brighter\",\"Add the word \\\"Required\\\" next to each, so meaning does not depend on color\",\"Remove the red\",\"Use green instead of red\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Never let color be the only cue. Adding a text label means the information survives when color is removed.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Body text is light gray on white. What is the check?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Eyeball it: it looks fine to me\",\"Switch to dark gray on white to clear the WCAG 4.5:1 contrast ratio for body text\",\"Make the font larger only\",\"Leave it, contrast does not matter on slides\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Body text must meet the 4.5:1 contrast ratio. Darkening the text clears the threshold for low-vision readers and bright-light conditions.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Four checks, four confirmable fixes. Accessibility is a repeatable checklist, not a judgment call, and each fix maps to a named WCAG check.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nOrder the pass so access is built in rather than bolted on.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Write functional alt text for every meaningful image\",\"Apply real heading styles to titles and sections\",\"Add a second cue wherever color carries meaning\",\"Check body-text contrast against the 4.5:1 ratio\",\"Map each fix to a named WCAG check a reviewer can confirm\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nFor every visual element you create, ask whether a person who cannot see it, cannot tell colors apart, or cannot read low-contrast text would still get the full meaning. If the answer is no, you have an accessibility gap to fix. The rule of thumb: never let a single channel (sight, color, contrast) be the only way to receive important information.\n\n1. **Image check:** Does every meaningful image have alt text that conveys its point?\n2. **Structure check:** Are titles and sections marked with real heading styles?\n3. **Independence check:** Is any information carried by color alone, and does the text meet the contrast ratio?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nA common mistake is writing alt text that describes the picture literally instead of its purpose. \"Image of a graph with blue bars\" tells a screen-reader user nothing useful; \"membership grew from 20 to 55 over the year\" tells them what the graph is for. Alt text answers \"why is this image here?\" not \"what does it look like?\"\n\nA second misreading is treating accessibility as a final cleanup step. Retrofitting access after the design is locked is slow and incomplete; you end up bolting alt text onto images and discovering that your color-coded system has no backup cue. Building access in from the start, the way Universal Design for Learning recommends, means the design works for everyone the first time instead of being patched for some people at the end.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine running an accessibility pass on a one-page event flyer you built in a word processor. Start with images: the venue map and the sponsor logos all need alt text, and the decorative divider line should be marked decorative so the screen reader skips it. Move to structure: the event name becomes `Heading 1`, the schedule and the RSVP sections become `Heading 2`, so a screen-reader user can jump between sections. Check color: the \"SOLD OUT\" sessions were shown only in gray, so add the word \"(full)\" next to each. Finally check contrast: the pale-blue tagline on white fails, so darken it.\n\nThe walkthrough shows accessibility as a repeatable checklist, not a judgment call. Each pass (images, structure, color, contrast) has a clear test, and each fix is something a reviewer or an automated checker can confirm. The flyer still looks like the same flyer; it is now usable by people whose needs you cannot see when you look at your own screen.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nWhen you review a document or slide for accessibility, do not ask \"does it look fine?\" Ask the four checks directly: turn on the screen reader or read the alt text aloud, view the page in grayscale to find color-only meaning, and run a contrast check on the body text. Each test exposes problems that are invisible to a normal glance.\n\nA strong reviewer of an artifact can answer four questions:\n\n1. Would a screen-reader user get every meaningful image and the page structure?\n2. Does any information vanish when color is removed?\n3. Does the body text meet the contrast threshold?\n4. Were these built in, or bolted on at the end?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nYour accessibility pass is strong when each fix maps to a named WCAG check and could be confirmed by a reviewer or a checker tool. The earlier layers are alt text, heading structure, color independence, and contrast; the last layer is visual polish, which should never come at the cost of access. If a fix is unclear, mark it in your trace and ask BigAL to help you write functional alt text or find the contrast ratio rather than asking it to make the slide \"look better.\"\n\nA useful note would read: \"On the Club Growth slide I rewrote the chart alt text to state the trend, applied a real Heading 1 to the title, added the word 'Required' so meaning does not rely on red, and darkened the body text to meet the contrast ratio. Each change maps to a WCAG check a reviewer can confirm.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nCarry the four checks to a different medium: a short video you post for a group project. The principles re-map cleanly. Alt text becomes captions and a transcript, so deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers get the audio content. Heading structure becomes clear on-screen titles and a described agenda, so the video is navigable. Color independence becomes \"do not say 'click the green button' without also naming it.\" Contrast applies to any on-screen text over video. The medium changed from a slide to a video, but the underlying question, can someone with a different way of perceiving still get the full meaning, did not. Owning that question rather than a fixed slide-checklist is what lets you make any artifact accessible.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/01-06-accessibility-basics-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"01.06\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show the artifact's accessibility gaps. The revised version should show each fix mapped to a WCAG check. The trace should explain how each fix helps a specific reader.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a slide with literal alt text against one with functional alt text.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the four checks to a new medium such as video.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes one of your fixes.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising an accessibility pass for CAT 100 01.06. A peer comment notes that an important status is shown only by color. Choose the next revision move and the second cue that would make the meaning survive without color.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_accessibility_basics\", \"accessibility\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_accessibility_basics\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your pass records alt text, heading structure, color independence, and contrast, each fix mapped to a WCAG check a reviewer could confirm.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_accessibility_basics","concept_ids":["accessibility","cat100_supplement_digital_citizenship_and_ai_readiness"],"stem":"Your slide has a chart showing club membership rising from 20 to 55. Which alt text is most useful to a screen reader user?","options":["IMG_2043.jpg","Bar chart showing club membership rising from 20 members in fall to 55 in spring.","image","chart"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Good alt text describes what the image shows and why it matters. A filename or the word 'image' tells the reader nothing.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_filename_as_alt","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_accessibility_basics","concept_ids":["accessibility"],"stem":"You want section titles in your document. You make each line 20pt bold so it looks like a heading. Why can this fail an accessibility check?","options":["Bold text is too hard to read on screens.","Screen readers and document navigation rely on real heading styles or tags, not on visual size, so bold-only titles are not recognized as headings.","Headings must always be underlined, not bold.","20pt is too small for any heading."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use real heading styles (Heading 1, Heading 2) or tags (h1, h2). Visual size alone does not create the structure assistive tools depend on.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_bold_not_heading","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","accessibility"],"stem":"You ask an AI tool to generate alt text for a complex diagram and it returns one vague line: \"A diagram about a process.\" How should you use this output responsibly?","options":["Use it as-is; AI alt text is always compliant.","Treat it as a draft, then rewrite it to name the actual steps shown, because you are responsible for whether the alt text is accurate.","Delete the diagram so you do not need alt text.","Ask the AI to make the alt text longer without checking accuracy."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"AI-generated alt text is a starting point. You must verify it describes the real content; vague output does not meet the need.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_filename_as_alt","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_accessibility_basics","concept_ids":["accessibility"],"stem":"A form marks required fields only by coloring their labels red. Why might this exclude some users, and what is the fix?","options":["Red is unprofessional; change it to blue.","Users who cannot distinguish red cannot tell which fields are required; add a text label or asterisk such as \"Required\" so meaning does not depend on color alone.","There is no problem if the red is bright enough.","Remove the colors and leave the fields unmarked."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Color alone can hide meaning from users who cannot distinguish it. Pair color with text or an icon so the cue is reliable.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_1_6_accessibility_basics_color_only_meaning","difficulty":"medium"}]}}